### What is method hiding vs overriding?

```java
public class Bank {
    public static String getType() { return "GENERIC"; }   // static
    public String getName() { return "National Bank"; }     // instance
}

public class SavingsBank extends Bank {
    public static String getType() { return "SAVINGS"; }    // HIDES parent static
    @Override
    public String getName() { return "Savings Division"; }  // OVERRIDES parent instance
}

Bank b = new SavingsBank();
System.out.println(b.getType());   // "GENERIC" – static: resolved by reference type (Bank)
System.out.println(b.getName());   // "Savings Division" – instance: resolved by object type (SavingsBank)
```

| Feature | Instance method | Static method |
|---|---|---|
| Resolved by | Object type (runtime) | Reference type (compile time) |
| @Override | Yes | No (compiler rejects) |
| Polymorphic? | Yes | No |
| Called via | object.method() | ClassName.method() |

### Can a subclass define the same static field?

Yes, but it creates a separate field (hiding), not an override:

```java
public class Bank {
    static String country = "INDIA";
}

public class InternationalBank extends Bank {
    static String country = "GLOBAL";  // hides, does not replace
}

System.out.println(Bank.country);              // INDIA
System.out.println(InternationalBank.country); // GLOBAL

Bank b = new InternationalBank();
System.out.println(b.country);  // INDIA (reference type is Bank)
```

Avoid this. It is confusing and error-prone.

### Can you put @Override on a static method?

```java
public class SavingsBank extends Bank {
    // @Override  // COMPILE ERROR: method does not override a superclass method
    public static String getType() { return "SAVINGS"; }
}
```

The compiler explicitly rejects @Override on static methods.

### What are common mistakes?

- **Assuming static methods are polymorphic.** `parent.staticMethod()` calls the parent's version, not the child's.
- **Hiding static fields in subclasses.** Creates two independent fields with the same name. Always access via the class name.
- **Using @Override on static.** It does not compile.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Design implication:** If you need polymorphic behavior, use instance methods. Static is the wrong tool for anything that needs to vary by subclass.
- **Factory method pattern:** `Account.create(type)` is static and chooses the subclass internally. This is a design choice, not polymorphism.
- **In Spring, @Bean methods are instance methods on @Configuration classes,** not static. Static @Bean methods exist but bypass the CGLIB proxy and lose some features.
